  <div class="wrap proof-band">
    <p class="log-intro band-intro">The real calls behind the system. The ones that went the wrong way are here too, set with the same care as the wins, because that is the part worth reading.</p>
    <ul class="proof" aria-label="What the record shows">
      <li data-outcome="reversal"><a href="#decision-grounding"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span><span class="proof-claim">Added retrieval grounding, cut it</span> <strong>fixed 0, broke 4</strong></a></li>
      <li data-outcome="reversal"><a href="#decision-model"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span><span class="proof-claim">Built the premium-model upgrade, declined it</span> <strong>0 rows at 1.97&times; the cost</strong></a></li>
      <li><a href="#decision-eval"><span class="proof-claim">Rebuilt the eval until accuracy <em>fell</em></span> <strong>97.3% &rarr; 88.9%</strong></a></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mark-legend"><span class="mark-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>A doubled rule marks a decision that was reversed.</p>
  </div>

  <main class="wrap" id="work" tabindex="-1">
    <section class="orientation" aria-label="What this record demonstrates">
      <p class="kicker">Start here</p>
      <p class="orientation-lede">A working record of how I build and run a small AI system, framed for whoever is reading: what it does for its user, and why every change had to earn its place on evidence.</p>
      <p class="orientation-routes">Read it as <a href="projects/product-and-program.html">Product &amp; Program</a>, go straight to the <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier</a>, or see the <a href="projects/faithfulness-judge.html">Judge</a>, where I caught my own measurement lying to me.</p>
    </section>

    <h2 class="section-title">The system</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">Three services wired into one loop: an API, an LLM classifier, and a RAG agent.</p>
    <figure class="diagram-wrap">
      <p class="flow-line" aria-label="System data flow, in order">
        Notes API <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        BackgroundTask <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        Classifier <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        Tags <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        Knowledge base <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        KB Agent <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        Grounded answer
      </p>
      <svg class="diagram" id="system-diagram" viewBox="0 0 800 430" role="group" aria-label="Interactive map of the system: notes-api, classifier, and kb-agent, connected by an async BackgroundTasks enrichment loop. Select a component to see what it does and the decision behind it.">
        <defs>
          <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <g id="diagram-edges"></g>
        <g id="diagram-nodes"></g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="diagram-detail" id="diagram-detail" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">Select a component to see what it does and the decision behind it.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="diagram-more"><a class="card-link" href="projects/the-system.html">Read the full writeup <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a></p>

    <section class="measure">
      <h2 class="section-title">The decision log</h2>

      <div class="decision" id="decision-grounding" data-outcome="reversal">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>BM25 retired<span class="code-ref">EVAL &middot; BM25</span></p>
        <h3>I added retrieval grounding, measured it, and cut it</h3>
        <p>I added BM25 lexical retrieval to ground each call, expecting a clear win. The first measurement said it barely helped. Then I found the measurement itself was unfair.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The result:</strong> I had been scoring the grounded arm against a stale baseline built on an older prompt. Rebuilt so both arms ran the same prompt, grounding fixed a domain call zero times and broke four across 162 classifications. It was retired outright, not left on as an option.</p>
        <p>An unfair baseline flatters whatever you were hoping for, and comparing two things means changing exactly one of them. The retrieval code stays in the repo, dormant and reproducible, because the built-it-measured-it-cut-it trail is the artifact. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html#decision-grounding">Full eval &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="decision-model" data-outcome="reversal">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>Model gated on evidence<span class="code-ref">SYS-002 &middot; ADR-013</span></p>
        <h3>Gate model choice on evidence, not vibes</h3>
        <p>Sonnet by default across the system. Escalate to Opus only where an eval shows the quality gain pays for itself. I built the upgrade, measured it, and declined it.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The result:</strong> routing the uncertain cases to the premium model moved <strong>zero rows</strong> on both axes at about 1.97&times; the cost per article. Sending everything to the premium model scored identically, so there was no headroom for any router to capture.</p>
        <p>A targeted prompt fix had already taken the ground routing was aimed at, so the cheapest fix won and that is now measured rather than asserted. The harness stays in the repo as the record. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Full verdict &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="decision-eval">
        <p class="kicker">Eval rebuilt<span class="code-ref">EVAL &middot; v1 &rarr; v3</span></p>
        <h3>I rebuilt the eval until it could tell me I was wrong</h3>
        <p>v1 graded the model on 300 snippets it generated itself, which measures consistency, not correctness. v2 replaced that with 54 hand-labeled real snippets, cross-checked by an Opus judge, and it has been catching me ever since.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The result:</strong> domain accuracy fell from 97.3% to 88.9% on real text, and that lower number is the trustworthy one.</p>
        <p>Category accuracy actually rose, because real vocabulary separates more cleanly; domain fell because the synthetic set was trivially easy to grade. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Full eval &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <p class="ledger-intro">Six more, in one line each. The full telling of every one is on the page it links to.</p>
      <ul class="ledger">
        <li id="decision-contracts">Decoupled the repos with frozen HTTP contracts &mdash; then audited the seam and found it <strong>broken with both repos' CI green</strong>, because each side asserted against its own copy of the shape. Two unit tests that happen to agree are not a contract test. <a href="projects/the-system.html">The System &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-falsegreen">Audited my own gates and found six of them reporting success for work that had not happened &mdash; a review lane that went green in <strong>36 seconds having posted nothing</strong>, and the counter built to catch that reading a key that does not exist. <a href="projects/false-green.html">False Green &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-truncation">Nearly published a dramatic premium-vs-cheap model gap that was <strong>my own bug</strong> &mdash; a <code>max_tokens</code> cap truncated the cheap tier before its verdict on 20% of claims, and my scoring counted each as a disagreement. <a href="projects/faithfulness-judge.html">Judge &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-ceiling">Called it a ceiling, then went looking anyway &mdash; the misses clustered on one boundary, and two rubric clauses moved category <strong>90.7% &rarr; 94.4%</strong> with nothing regressing. A prompt problem wearing a ceiling's clothes. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-prompt" data-outcome="reversal"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>A prompt that read better to a human and measured worse &mdash; category <strong>79.0% &rarr; 76.7%</strong>, industry recall 0.217 &rarr; 0.100. Reverted. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-rightsized" data-outcome="reversal"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>Built the enrichment loop on Kafka to learn it properly, then cut it &mdash; a single-user idempotent workload <strong>never needed a distributed log</strong>. Now an in-process background task. <a href="projects/the-system.html">The System &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-tooluse">Forced output to a tool schema instead of parsing prose, so an invalid label is rejected at the API layer &mdash; <strong>one out-of-enum response in 300</strong>, handled by a re-sample. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier &rarr;</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="measure">
      <h2 class="section-title">Classifier evidence</h2>
      <p>Where the classifier stands now, on 54 hand-labeled real snippets.</p>
      <ul class="stat-strip">
        <li><span class="stat-value" data-metric="category_accuracy">92.6%</span><span class="stat-label">Category</span></li>
        <li><span class="stat-value" data-metric="domain_accuracy">92.6%</span><span class="stat-label">Domain</span></li>
        <li><span class="stat-value" data-metric="region_accuracy">87.0%</span><span class="stat-label">Region</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="stat-note">Category and domain land on the same figure here. Two axes, scored separately, not a copied cell.</p>
      <p>Those are the numbers after the eval got harder, not before. The full trajectory &mdash; synthetic self-graded, then real hand-labeled, a model migration, a prompt fix and a third axis, with the ceiling I called wrongly and the misses that clustered on one boundary &mdash; is the table on the classifier page. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">See the full eval &rarr;</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="measure">
      <h2 class="section-title">Field notes</h2>
      <p>Notes I keep while learning, in plain language, on the techniques behind all of this. <a href="https://sanlee-ys.github.io/learning-notes/">Read them &rarr;</a></p>
      <ul class="notes-list">
        <li>Structured output via tool-use</li>
        <li>Eval-driven development</li>
